        "question": {
          ":question/id": "c62263a8-4734-51b4-ba23-ac66e63275c4",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "![Ellipse with center guides, rightmost point A and a counterclockwise arrow.](/tmp/ca9837-templates-5ek4tgt5/17592186152189/ellipse-860d16ac144de82f4baf2ba6c63d5b22fb84363fc86acc6ead29b6db01d413c9.png)\n\nAn ellipse has center $(-6,3)$, horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $3$.\n\nConstruct parametric equations for one counterclockwise traversal, starting at $A(-2,3)$ when $\\theta=0$, with $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$.\n\nUse sine or cosine of $\\theta$, without changing its argument.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}",
          ":question/worked-solution": "An ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=-6$, $k=3$, $a=4$ and $b=3$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta-6,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta+3,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nCheck the starting point and direction at successive quarter turns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0 &: (-2,3)\\\\\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2} &: (-6,6)\\\\\\theta=\\pi &: (-10,3)\\\\\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2} &: (-6,0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe path goes right, top, left, bottom: counterclockwise. The scaled and translated unit circle makes one full traversal; excluding $2\\pi$ avoids repeating the starting point.",
