          ":question/problem": "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](/tmp/ca9837-templates-5ek4tgt5/17592186152189/ellipse-bead4465799c7a037d805f01d2d7da2085f46dde00cf039fc90721378a606e89.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The horizontal extremes are $-10$ and $-2$; the vertical extremes are $-6$ and $0$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-10+(-2)}{2}=-6, &k&=\\frac{-6+(0)}{2}=-3,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{-2-(-10)}{2}=4, &b&=\\frac{0-(-6)}{2}=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=-6$, $k=-3$, $a=4$ and $b=3$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta-6,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta-3,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$",
